$\begin{aligned}
& A=\left[\begin{array}{ll}
1 & 2 \\
2 & 3
\end{array}\right] \\
& A^2=\left[\begin{array}{ll}
1 & 2 \\
2 & 3
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 2 \\
2 & 3
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1+4 & 2+6 \\
2+6 & 4+9
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
5 & 8 \\
8 & 13
\end{array}\right] \\
& \mathrm{A}^2-\mathrm{kA}-\mathrm{I}_2=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
5 & 8 \\
8 & 13
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mathrm{k} & 2 \mathrm{k} \\
2 \mathrm{k} & 3 \mathrm{k}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right] \\
& =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
5-\mathrm{k}-1 & 8-2 \mathrm{k}-0 \\
8-2 \mathrm{k}-0 & 13-3 \mathrm{k}-1
\end{array}\right] \\
& =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
4-\mathrm{k} & 8-2 \mathrm{k} \\
8-2 \mathrm{k} & 12-3 \mathrm{k}
\end{array}\right]
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned}
& \text{ Given, } \mathrm{A}^2-\mathrm{kA}-\mathrm{I}_2=0 \\
& \therefore 4-\mathrm{k}=0 \Rightarrow \mathrm{k}=4
\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned} & \text{ Let }[A]=\left[\begin{array}{ll}a & b \\ c & d\end{array}\right] \\ & \begin{aligned} A^{-1} & =\frac{1}{|A|}(a d j . A) \\ & =\frac{1}{(a d-b c)}\left[\begin{array}{cc}d & -c \\ -b & a\end{array}\right]^T=\frac{1}{(a d-b c)}\left[\begin{array}{cc}d & -b \\ -c & a\end{array}\right]\end{aligned}\end{aligned}\begin{aligned}
& \operatorname{adj}\left(A^{-1}\right)=\frac{1}{(a d-b c)}\left[\begin{array}{ll}
a & c \\
b & d
\end{array}\right]^T=\frac{1}{(a d-b c)}\left[\begin{array}{ll}
a & b \\
c & d
\end{array}\right]...(1) \\
& \operatorname{adj} A=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
d & -b \\
-c & a
\end{array}\right] \\
& |\operatorname{adj} A|=(a d-b c) \\
& (\operatorname{adj} A)^{-1}=\frac{1}{\mid a d j} A \left\lvert\,\left[\begin{array}{ll}
a & c \\
b & d
\end{array}\right]^T=\frac{1}{a d-b c}\left[\begin{array}{ll}
a & b \\
c & d
\end{array}\right] .\right....(2)
\end{aligned}$
Subtracting eqn. (1) and (2),
$\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{adj}\left(A^{-1}\right)-(\operatorname{adj} A)^{-1} & =\left[\begin{array}{ll}
0 & 0 \\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right] \\
& =\text{ null matrix. }
\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{A}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mathrm{x}+\mathrm{y} & \mathrm{y} \\
2 \mathrm{x} & \mathrm{x}-\mathrm{y}
\end{array}\right] \\
& \mathrm{B}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
2 \\
-1
\end{array}\right] \text{ and } \mathrm{C}=\left[\begin{array}{l}
3 \\
2
\end{array}\right]
\end{aligned}$
Here $\mathrm{AB}=\mathrm{C}\begin{aligned}
\therefore & {\left[\begin{array}{cc}
x+y & y \\
2 x & x-y
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
2 \\
-1
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{l}
3 \\
2
\end{array}\right] } \\
\Rightarrow & {\left[\begin{array}{cc}
2(x+y) & -y \\
4 x & -x+y
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{l}
3 \\
2
\end{array}\right] } \\
& 2 x+y=3 ...(i)\\
& 3 x+y=2...(ii)
\end{aligned}$
From equations (i) and (ii), we get $x=-1$ and $y=5\begin{aligned}
\therefore & A=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
4 & 5 \\
-2 & -6
\end{array}\right] \\
& \text{ Now, } A^2=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
4 & 5 \\
-2 & -6
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
4 & 5 \\
-2 & -6
\end{array}\right] \\
& =\left[\begin{array}{cc}
16-10 & 20-30 \\
-8+12 & -10+36
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
6 & -10 \\
4 & 26
\end{array}\right]
\end{aligned}\therefore \quad$ Option (A) is correct.

